## The protocol

A plugin is an ordinary executable. {{ host }} runs it twice:

```console
$ {{ bin_name }} --manifest   # what it offers, as JSON on stdout
$ {{ bin_name }} run          # invocation as JSON on stdin, outcome on stdout
```

The manifest declares the protocol version, a name, and the commands this
plugin contributes:

```jsonc
{
  "protocol_version": {{ protocol_version }},
  "name": "{{ name }}",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "commands": [
    { "key": "{{ command_key }}", "label": "{{ command_label }}", "target": "{{ target }}" }
  ]
}
```

An invocation arrives as `{"command": "{{ command_key }}", "target": "{{ target }}", "subject": {…}}`,
where `subject` is {{ subject_about }}. The reply may carry `meta` (merged into
the row - a plugin adds and overwrites its own keys but never clears the rest),
`message` (shown to the user), or `error`.

Plugins live for the duration of one call. Nothing here starts a service.

## Developing

```console
$ cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo test
```

That is the same gate CI runs, so a green terminal means a green pull request.

`tests/protocol.rs` runs the built binary the way {{ host }} runs it and holds
the answers to what the host accepts - a plugin that passes its unit tests and
fails there is one {{ host }} would list and refuse.

## Layout

| Path | What lives there |
| --- | --- |
| `src/main.rs` | The manifest and the plugin's own work |
| `src/protocol.rs` | The wire: an invocation in, an outcome out |
| `tests/protocol.rs` | The plugin run as the host runs it |
| `docs/adr/` | Architecture decisions, newest last |

